HST Office: saving over an existing file never leaves it empty or cut short.

- sfx2 docfile.cxx TransactedTransferForFS_Impl: the temporary file is fsync'd before the rename that
  replaces the user's file and the folder is fsync'd after it (before, the file was synced only after the
  rename, the folder never). Where upstream truncates the file to 0 bytes and rewrites it in place -- a
  symbolic link on Linux, every file on macOS (Finder's "hide extension" flag) -- the real file is now
  replaced through a temporary file in its own folder that gets its permissions, owner (when allowed) and
  extended attributes (macOS: resource fork, Finder info, ACLs), fsync and rename; the link stays a link.
  Hard links, read-only files and failures keep the upstream in-place write.
- The temporary file next to the user's file is named .~hst-save-<pid>-<n>.tmp; a later save in that folder
  removes ones whose process is gone and that are older than 10 minutes (any after 7 days: shared folders).
- hstsavefile.{hxx,cxx}, hstsavetemps.cxx: the helpers.

License: these upstream files remain under their original license (MPL-2.0).
Test: tests/unit/test_recovery_durability_patches.py; tests/runtime/check-save-replace.py (Linux symlink;
macOS on the macOS build).
